# 17 The free surface: geometric VOF advection {#ch:vof}

## Volume fractions and their reconstruction

Each cell stores $f\in[0,1]$, the fraction of its volume occupied by water.
$f$ alone does not say where the water is inside the cell. Before moving it,
`vof.h` reconstructs the interface in each cut cell ($0 < f < 1$) as a
straight line (a plane in 3D), the PLIC method (piecewise-linear interface
calculation \[[13](references)\]). Figure 17.1 shows the result on a
circle.

![Figure 17.1: PLIC reconstruction of a circle (dashed) on a $6\times6$ grid,
computed for this document with the Youngs normal and Basilisk's
`line_alpha()`. Numbers are the volume fractions. Each red segment is
the line that cuts off exactly the area $f$ in its cell with the estimated
normal. The segments do not join up: PLIC is discontinuous from cell to
cell, but it conserves the volume in each cell exactly.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_plic.png)

**The line in a cell.** In coordinates scaled to the unit cell
$[-\tfrac{1}{2},\tfrac{1}{2}]^d$, the interface is
$$
\mathbf{n}\cdot\mathbf{x}= \alpha,
$$
with $\mathbf{n}$ pointing out of the water and the water on the side
$\mathbf{n}\cdot\mathbf{x}< \alpha$. Two numbers are needed: the normal $\mathbf{n}$ and the
intercept $\alpha$.

**The normal: Mixed-Youngs-Centred (MYC).** Youngs' method takes
$\mathbf{n}\propto -\nabla f$ from the $3\times3$ block of cells with weights
$1,2,1$:
$$
n_x \propto (f_{i-1,j+1} + 2f_{i-1,j} + f_{i-1,j-1}) - (f_{i+1,j+1} + 2f_{i+1,j} + f_{i+1,j-1}).
$$
The centred method uses column sums, which are height functions: if the
interface is closer to horizontal, $n_x \propto \tfrac12(c_{\text{left}} -
c_{\text{right}})$ with $c$ the sum of $f$ in the left and right columns, and
$n_y = \pm1$. Neither is best everywhere. `mycs()` computes both and
keeps the one with the larger slope (minor component over major component),
then normalises so that $|n_x| + |n_y| = 1$ \[[13](references)\].

**The intercept.** Given $\mathbf{n}$, the area cut off by the line is a
monotone function of $\alpha$, so there is exactly one $\alpha$ for each $f$.
In 2D, with $n_1 = \min(|n_x|,|n_y|)$, $n_2 = \max$ and $\alpha$ measured
from the corner of the cell where the water region starts, the water region
is a triangle while $\alpha \le n_1$ and a trapezoid after that, so
$$
f = \begin{cases}
\dfrac{\alpha^2}{2n_1n_2} & \alpha \le n_1,\\[2mm]
\dfrac{\alpha - n_1/2}{n_2} & n_1 \le \alpha \le n_2,\\[2mm]
1 - \dfrac{(n_1 + n_2 - \alpha)^2}{2n_1n_2} & \alpha \ge n_2,
\end{cases}
\qquad
\alpha = \begin{cases}
\sqrt{2fn_1n_2},\\
fn_2 + n_1/2,\\
n_1 + n_2 - \sqrt{2n_1n_2(1 - f)}.
\end{cases}
$$
`line_alpha()` evaluates the right-hand inversion, then shifts $\alpha$
to the cell-centred coordinates. `plane_alpha()` does the same in 3D,
where the cases are more numerous and involve a cubic, solved with the
trigonometric formula.

![Figure 17.2: The PLIC interface in a unit cell centred at the origin. The normal
points from water to air. Given $\mathbf{n}$, $\alpha$ is the unique value that
makes the shaded area equal to $f$.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_plic-cell.png)

## Direction-split advection {#sec:split}

In 3D, computing the exact volume swept through a face by a general
velocity field is complicated. `vof.h` instead advects in one
direction at a time. In a single $x$ sweep the velocity field $(u, 0, 0)$ is
not divergence-free, so the sweep solves the 1D form of
$\partial_t f + \mathbf{u}\cdot\nabla f = 0$ written with a flux:
$$
\partial_t f + \partial_x(u f) = f\,\partial_x u.
\qquad (17.4)
$$
The right-hand side is the *compression term*. Discretised over one
sweep:
$$
f_i \leftarrow f_i + \dfrac{\Delta t}{c_{m}\Delta}\left[F_{i-\dfrac 12}- F_{i+\dfrac 12}+ \chi_i\left(u_{f,{i+\dfrac 12}} - u_{f,{i-\dfrac 12}}\right)\right].
\qquad (17.5)
$$
The sweeps run in the order $x, y, z$ on even steps and rotate on odd steps
(`sweep[(i + d) % dimension]`), so the splitting error does not
always favour the same direction.

## The geometric flux {#sec:vofflux}

During one sweep, the fluid that crosses face ${i+\dfrac 12}$ is the strip of the
upwind cell of width $|u|\Delta t$ next to the face (figure 17.3). The
volume of water in that strip is computed exactly from the reconstructed
line:
$$
F_{i+\dfrac 12}= c_f\,u_{f,{i+\dfrac 12}},\qquad
c_f = \text{fraction of the strip below the line}.
$$
`rectangle_fraction()` computes $c_f$ by rescaling the strip to a unit
square and calling `plane_volume()` (the forward function $f(\alpha)$
above). If the upwind cell is full or empty, $c_f$ is 1 or 0.

![Figure 17.3: Geometric VOF flux. The hatched strip is the volume that crosses the
face in one sweep. $c_f$ is the part of it under the reconstructed interface,
computed exactly from $\mathbf{n}$ and $\alpha$.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_vofflux.png)

> **In the code: `vof.h`, `sweep_x()`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> double un = uf.x[]*dt/(Delta*fm.x[] + SEPS), s = sign(un);
> int i = -(s + 1.)/2.;
> double cf = (c[i] <= 0. || c[i] >= 1.) ? c[i] :
>   rectangle_fraction ((coord){-s*n.x[i], n.y[i], n.z[i]}, alpha[i],
>                       (coord){-0.5, -0.5, -0.5}, (coord){s*un - 0.5, 0.5, 0.5});
> flux[] = cf*uf.x[];
> ...
> c[] += dt*(flux[] - flux[1] + cc[]*(uf.x[1] - uf.x[]))/(cm[]*Delta);
> ~~~
>
> The sign flip $-s\,n_x$ mirrors the cell so that the strip is always at the
> low-$x$ end of the unit square.

## Exact conservation: the Weymouth--Yue step function {#sec:wy}

What value of $f$ goes in the compression term? Weymouth and Yue
\[[14](references)\] showed that the choice
$$
\chi_i = \begin{cases}1 & f_i^n > 0.5,\\ 0 & \text{otherwise},\end{cases}
$$
computed once before the first sweep and kept fixed for all sweeps, makes
the scheme conserve volume exactly. Sum (17.5) over all
sweeps and all cells. The flux differences telescope to the fluxes through
the domain boundary. The compression terms add up, for each cell, to
$$
\dfrac{\Delta t}{\Delta}\,\chi_i\sum_d\left(u_{f,{i+\dfrac 12}} - u_{f,{i-\dfrac 12}}\right)_d = \Delta t\,\chi_i\,(\mathcal{D}\mathbf{u}_f)_i = 0,
$$
because $\chi_i$ is the same in every sweep and $\mathbf{u}_f$ is discretely
divergence-free after the projection ([chapter 15](15_projection)). Total water
volume therefore changes only by what crosses the domain boundary, to the
tolerance of the pressure solve. Weymouth and Yue also show that with
$\chi$ a step function and $C \le \tfrac{1}{2}$ the sweeps keep $f$ within $[0,1]$
up to round-off.

## Why VOF needs CFL $\le 0.5$ {#sec:vofcfl}

In one sweep a cell can lose fluid through both faces at once (when the flow
diverges in the 1D sweep). Each outgoing strip has width $|u|\Delta t$ inside the
cell. If $|u|\Delta t\le \Delta/2$ on both sides, the two strips cannot overlap and
the cell cannot give away more than it holds. This is the condition used by
the boundedness argument above. `vof.h` lowers the solver's CFL number
to 0.5 and prints a warning whenever a face exceeds it.

## With an embedded solid

With `embed.h`, the update is multiplied by $c_s$:
`c[] += dt*cs[]*(flux[] - flux[1] + …)/(cm[]*Delta)`. Since $c_m =
c_s$, this means the solid part of a cut cell is ignored and $f$ is the
fraction of the *whole* cell, not of its fluid part. The authors chose
this because it keeps $f$ bounded and conserves volume when the volume is
also measured ignoring the solid.

## Initial and boundary values of $f$

`fraction(f, phi)` initialises $f$ from a level-set function $\phi$
($\phi > 0$ inside): $\phi$ is evaluated at cell vertices, the edge
intersections give face fractions, and the face fractions give the volume
fraction. On the adaptive mesh, $f$ is refined with
`fraction_refine()`: the parent's interface is reconstructed and each
child receives the exact area of the line in its quarter.

